---
import Presentation from "../../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Quarter = `WHAT QUARTER 3 WAS

  53-64   the Create Performance Task
          30% of your AP exam grade, submitted

  65-69   Computer Systems and Networks
          11-15% of the multiple-choice section

  70-77   Impact of Computing
          21-26% of the multiple-choice section

Between them, the two content blocks are about
a third of the multiple-choice paper.`;

const Card = `THE CARD -- everything worth carrying into May

NETWORKS
  computing device        can run a program
  computing system        devices + programs, one purpose
  computer network        a TYPE of computing system
  path                    a sequence of directly
                          connected devices
  routing                 the PROCESS of finding a path
  bandwidth               max data in a fixed time,
                          in bits per second
  protocol                an agreed-upon set of rules
  open                    non-proprietary, published
  scalability             capacity to change in size
                          to meet new demands
  packet                  a chunk of data + metadata
                          for routing AND reassembly
  packets may arrive      in order, out of order,
                          or not at all
  IP TCP UDP              the three named protocols
  the Web                 linked pages, programs, files
                          -- it USES the internet
  redundancy              extra components to mitigate
                          failure
  fault tolerant          supports failures and still
                          functions
  sequential              sum of all steps
  parallel                sequential tasks + longest
                          parallel task
                          (if every task gets a processor)
  distributed             multiple DEVICES
  speedup                 sequential / parallel

IMPACT
  computing innovation    includes a program as an
                          integral part of its function
  identification          classification AND justification
  one effect              can be beneficial AND harmful
  digital divide          differing access, by socioeconomic,
                          geographic, demographic
                          -- groups AND individuals
                          -- globally AND locally
  it raises               equity, access, influence
  affected by             individuals, organizations,
                          governments
  bias enters via         the ALGORITHM or the DATA
                          -- at all levels
  crowdsourcing           input from many people via
                          the internet
  citizen science         research by distributed
                          individuals, using THEIR OWN
                          devices
  plagiarism              without permission AND
                          presented as one's own
  always cite             material created by others
  Creative Commons        a license a creator applies
  open source             programs
  open access             research output
  PII                     identifies, links, relates to,
                          or DESCRIBES an individual
  aggregation             disparate data combined to
                          create knowledge about a person
  strong password         hard to guess BASED ON
                          KNOWLEDGE OF THAT USER
  multifactor             2+ of knowledge, possession,
                          inherence
  symmetric               one key for both
  public key              public encrypts, private decrypts
  certificate authority   validates key ownership,
                          on a TRUST MODEL
  malware                 damages or takes partial control
  virus                   malware that COPIES ITSELF
  phishing                tricks a user into providing
                          information
  keylogging              records every keystroke
  rogue access point      unauthorized access to a
                          secure network
  all systems             have exploitable flaws`;

const Q4 = `QUARTER 4 -- WHAT IT IS

  MCQ practice by Big Idea, weighted ABOVE session
    share for Data and Impact of Computing

  all three question formats
    single-select, multi-select, reading passage

  written response practice against YOUR OWN
    Personalized Project Reference

  full timed practice exams, at least two

  targeted reteach, driven by your results

Nothing new is taught. Everything is rehearsed.`;

const WhatYouOwn = `AND ONE THING YOU ALREADY OWN

  Your Personalized Project Reference is submitted.
  It is four code segments you chose.

  In May you answer four questions about it,
  from memory, in sixty minutes.

  That is FOUR OF YOUR SIX Create Task points --
  and Quarter 4 is where you practice them.`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Activity 3.19: Quarter 3 Review</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Brief -->
        <section id="brief">
          <h2>Three Things Today</h2>

          <p>
            <b>A review, the quarter checkpoint, and a look at Quarter 4.</b> The
            checkpoint is 40 points and covers sessions 65 to 77 &mdash; not the Create
            Task, which was already assessed.
          </p>

          <Code code={Quarter} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Where the Quarter Went" showLineNumbers={false} class="my-4" />
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Launch -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>Activity 3.19</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">Quarter 3 Review</h3>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>What This Quarter Was</h2>
              <Code code={Quarter} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Three Blocks" showLineNumbers={false} />
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>The Thing to Notice</h2>
              <p>You wrote no new programs after session 64.</p>
              <p class="fragment">
                And you covered <b>about a third of the multiple-choice paper.</b>
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">
                <b class="text-sn-theme">This is the part of the exam that is won by
                precision with words.</b>
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Three Arguments Made Repeatedly</h2>
              <p><b>More of a resource does not fix a structural limit.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">More data does not fix bias. More processors do not shorten the sequential portion.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Sessions 44, 69, and 72.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Two</h2>
              <p><b>Openness is a feature and a vulnerability, and it is the same property.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">Open protocols let the internet grow &mdash; and let anyone connect.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Open contribution makes crowdsourcing work &mdash; and lets anyone contribute badly.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Three</h2>
              <p><b>A correct program can still be the wrong thing to ship.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">The salary program had no defect in it.</p>
              <p class="fragment">The anonymized data was correctly anonymized and identified four people.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>What the Checkpoint Asks</h2>
              <p>Definitions, precisely. <b>Two calculations.</b> And two written answers.</p>
              <p class="fragment">
                The written ones are scored the way the exam scores them:
                <b> classification and justification.</b>
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>And Then Quarter 4</h2>
              <Code code={Q4} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Nothing New" showLineNumbers={false} />
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>The Part You Should Care About</h2>
              <Code code={WhatYouOwn} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Four of Six" showLineNumbers={false} />
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>One Honest Warning</h2>
              <p>You submitted your Project Reference weeks ago.</p>
              <p class="fragment">
                <b>In May you will have forgotten how your own code works.</b>
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">Everybody does. That is what Quarter 4 is for.</p>
            </section>
          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- The card -->
        <section id="card">
          <h2>The Card</h2>

          <p>
            <b>Everything from sessions 65 to 77 worth carrying into May.</b> Copy it,
            print it, or rewrite it in your own words &mdash; the last of those is worth
            the most.
          </p>

          <Code code={Card} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Quarter 3, On One Page" showLineNumbers={false} class="mb-3" />
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Self diagnostic -->
        <section id="diagnostic">
          <h2>Before the Checkpoint</h2>

          <p>
            <b>Mark anything you cannot do without looking it up. Whatever you mark is
            your revision.</b>
          </p>

          <h5 class="mt-4">Networks</h5>
          <ul>
            <li>Distinguish a computing device, a computing system and a computer network &mdash; <b>3.6</b></li>
            <li>Say what routing is, without describing a path &mdash; <b>3.6</b></li>
            <li>Give the units of bandwidth &mdash; <b>3.6</b></li>
            <li>Say what <i>open</i> means and why it let the internet scale &mdash; <b>3.7</b></li>
            <li>Define scalability &mdash; <b>3.7</b></li>
            <li>Say what packet metadata is for &mdash; both jobs &mdash; <b>3.8</b></li>
            <li>Name the three things that can happen to a packet &mdash; <b>3.8</b></li>
            <li>Choose between TCP and UDP and justify it &mdash; <b>3.8</b></li>
            <li>Distinguish the internet from the World Wide Web &mdash; <b>3.8</b></li>
            <li>Define redundancy and fault tolerance, and say which causes which &mdash; <b>3.9</b></li>
            <li><b>Identify a single point of failure in a diagram</b> &mdash; <b>3.9</b></li>
            <li>Calculate sequential and parallel times <b>with a processor limit</b> &mdash; <b>3.10</b></li>
            <li>Calculate speedup, the right way up &mdash; <b>3.10</b></li>
            <li>Say why more processors stop helping &mdash; <b>3.10</b></li>
          </ul>

          <h5 class="mt-4">Impact</h5>
          <ul>
            <li>Say what makes something a computing innovation &mdash; <b>3.11</b></li>
            <li><b>Give a classification and a justification, naming who</b> &mdash; <b>3.11</b></li>
            <li>Explain how one effect is both beneficial and harmful &mdash; <b>3.11</b></li>
            <li>Define the digital divide with <b>all three</b> characteristics &mdash; <b>3.12</b></li>
            <li>Name the three issues it raises and the three actors &mdash; <b>3.12</b></li>
            <li>Name <b>both</b> sources of bias &mdash; <b>3.13</b></li>
            <li>Explain how a correct program can be biased &mdash; <b>3.13</b></li>
            <li>Define crowdsourcing and citizen science &mdash; <b>3.14</b></li>
            <li>Judge whether a task suits a crowd &mdash; <b>3.14</b></li>
            <li>Give <b>both</b> conditions for plagiarism &mdash; <b>3.15</b></li>
            <li>Distinguish Creative Commons, open source and open access &mdash; <b>3.15</b></li>
            <li>Define PII, including all four verbs &mdash; <b>3.16</b></li>
            <li><b>Explain aggregation</b> &mdash; <b>3.16</b></li>
            <li>Say what makes a password strong, in the exam's terms &mdash; <b>3.17</b></li>
            <li>Count authentication factors correctly &mdash; <b>3.17</b></li>
            <li>Say which key encrypts and which decrypts &mdash; <b>3.17</b></li>
            <li>Distinguish malware from a virus &mdash; <b>3.18</b></li>
            <li>Define phishing, keylogging and a rogue access point &mdash; <b>3.18</b></li>
          </ul>

          <p class="mt-3">
            <b>How many did you mark?</b> ______
          </p>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">The three in bold are the ones students most often
            cannot do under time pressure.</b> If you marked any of them, start there.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Reflection -->
        <section id="reflection">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>

          <p>Answer these after the checkpoint.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>You spent ten sessions on the Create Task with no feedback from anyone. Name one thing that made that harder than you expected, and one thing that made it easier.</li>
            <li>Sessions 65 to 77 had no programming in them at all. Did that change how you studied? Be honest.</li>
            <li>In May you answer four questions about code you wrote months ago. <b>Which of the four worries you most</b>, and what would you want to practice first in Quarter 4?</li>
          </ol>

          <p class="mt-3">
            <b>Question 3's answers decide what Quarter 4 spends its time on.</b> Answer
            it properly.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>Your marked diagnostic and your reflection.</p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Quarter Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
